This term not only means to go, to depart, but also to be liberated, to go forth in freedom.

cue0118797
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:21.000
It is the same term usually translated as Exodus describing Israel's departure from the house of bondage in Egypt.

cue0118798
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:42.012
Within the context of our story it means that Abraham and Śara when well advanced in years he was 75 and she was 65 years old broke away from the system of values enslaving them and started a new life

cue0118799
00:02:42.012 --> 00:02:49.012
A life that rejuvenated them both spiritually and biologically.

cue0118800
00:02:49.012 --> 00:03:12.012
Abraham lived 100 years more. And Śara 62. Indeed, about a thousand years later, when the people of Israel were in material and spiritual decline, Isaiah calls forth the figures of Abraham and Śara to inspire them to overcome their despair.

cue0118801
00:03:12.012 --> 00:03:20.012
Look into the rock from where you were young and to the pit from where you were digged.

cue0118802
00:03:20.012 --> 00:03:29.012
Look into Abraham your father and into Śara that bare you, for he was alone when I called him.

cue0118803
00:03:29.012 --> 00:03:39.012
And I blessed him, that is spiritually, and increased him, that is materially.

cue0118804
00:03:39.012 --> 00:03:47.052
In order to grasp Abraham's spiritual bondage, it will be necessary to consider some of the

cue0118805
00:03:47.052 --> 00:03:53.292
essential features of Mesopotamian civilization.

cue0118806
00:03:53.292 --> 00:04:03.612
Since early antiquity, there were two dominant civilizations flourishing, one in Egypt and

cue0118807
00:04:03.612 --> 00:04:13.212
And the other in Mesopotamia, with totally different philosophies of men and society.

cue0118808
00:04:13.212 --> 00:04:23.872
Mesopotamia rejected the idea of one single omnipotent God. Individually, the gods of

cue0118809
00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:34.592
The Babylonian pantheon held only limited power. Ultimate power resided in the totality

cue0118810
00:04:34.592 --> 00:04:41.152
of the gods. It was the effect of different configurations and alliances established by

cue0118811
00:04:41.152 --> 00:04:53.632
the gods and therefore subject to manipulation. These alliances were revised every year, giving

cue0118812
00:04:53.632 --> 00:05:06.204
Rays to new configurations of power and ideologies This concept of religion was a replica of the political system

cue0118813
00:05:06.204 --> 00:05:12.344
The Mesopotamian king did not represent absolute power.

cue0118814
00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:19.504
The authority of the king was limited by the power of the gods above and the consensus

cue0118815
00:05:19.504 --> 00:05:48.504
The elders below to whom he was accountable. They also conceived of a cosmic and impersonal law called Kitum, to which even the king was its humble servant. In this way, the rights of the individual were protected and the abuses of authority checked.

cue0118816
00:05:49.504 --> 00:06:01.504
In many significant aspects, the Mesopotamian society resembled our own modern society in the West.

cue0118817
00:06:01.504 --> 00:06:10.504
Scholars have pointed out our debt to Mesopotamian civilization, particularly in the fields of government.

cue0118818
00:06:10.504 --> 00:06:17.504
Democracy seems to have been a Mesopotamian invention. Law and science.

cue0118819
00:06:17.504 --> 00:06:42.504
Perhaps this is why it is so easy for us to understand the frustration of a 75 years old man at the twilight of his life with the melodies of a society that produces spiritual anarchy, intellectual relativism, social apathy and bewilderment.

cue0118820
00:06:42.504 --> 00:07:04.504
A distinguished ancient Near Eastern scholar observed, the collapse of Nineveh and Babylon was due not so much to the superiority of outside powers as to the crushing weight of the internal structure.

cue0118821
00:07:04.504 --> 00:07:17.364
The Tower of Baḇel is the symbol of a society without absolutes. It represents the society

cue0118822
00:07:17.364 --> 00:07:37.036
in which genuine human communication is excluded The individual is condemned to existential The solitude deafened by the tumult of languages he cannot possibly understand

cue0118823
00:07:58.876 --> 00:08:02.876
society and goes searching for the promised land.

cue0118824
00:08:05.916 --> 00:08:17.196
In his journey away from Mesopotamia, Abraham is stretching his hand to everyone who can feel the

cue0118825
00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:23.356
anguish of an individual without ultimate goals and aspirations.

cue0118826
00:08:25.036 --> 00:08:32.036
Let us hold his hand and follow his footsteps into the Promised Land.

cue0118827
00:08:37.516 --> 00:08:44.516
At the time of the Patriarchs, the land of Canaan was a scarcely populated area inhabited

cue0118828
00:08:44.796 --> 00:08:51.796
by people of Semitic and non-Semitic background. Both economically and culturally, it was a

cue0118829
00:08:55.036 --> 00:09:19.836
The first

cue0118830
00:09:19.836 --> 00:09:31.776
Mesopotamia. To begin with, although Egypt was strongly polytheistic, it believed essentially

cue0118831
00:09:31.776 --> 00:09:42.576
in one single power for the entire cosmos. This power was perpetually incarnated in the

cue0118832
00:09:42.576 --> 00:10:00.056
The Pharaoh was divine and a depositary of absolute power. He was above all and accountable to none.

cue0118833
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:11.140
Within such a system, no laws or rights can be truly operative. As the great Egyptologist

cue0118834
00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:19.940
John A. Wilson remarked, the authority of codified law would have competed with the

cue0118835
00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:28.960
personal authority of the Pharaoh. The people of Egypt, regardless of their social status

cue0118836
00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:52.680
The

cue0118837
00:10:52.680 --> 00:11:04.640
In the annals of history, as slaves of Pharaoh, the individuals had no rights, but they enjoyed

cue0118838
00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:17.020
absolute stability. In Egypt, time was static. There was no real past or future. Changes

cue0118839
00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:26.520
These were superficial disturbances of the established order. The present was overwhelming

cue0118840
00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:38.780
and irremovable. In a profound sense, ancient Egypt is an excellent illustration of totalitarianism

cue0118841
00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:42.780
in its most developed form.

cue0118842
00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:53.780
Within the structure of Egyptian thought, monotheism is possible.

cue0118843
00:11:53.780 --> 00:12:06.100
Indeed, upon his return to the land of Canaan, Abraham met Melchisedek, the king and priest

cue0118844
00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:35.994
The first monotheistic formula in biblical literature He referred to God as the Most High God Creator of Heaven and Earth Thereupon Abraham adopted this formula and used it when giving an oath to the king of Sodom

cue0118845
00:12:35.994 --> 00:12:46.994
This formula is highly significant. As we shall see, it expresses Egyptian ideology.

cue0118846
00:12:46.994 --> 00:13:00.754
The term Qone that we translated Creator means also Possessor, Master. The meaning is clear.

cue0118847
00:13:00.754 --> 00:13:11.234
God, as the Creator of the Universe, is also its absolute Master and Possessor. Within

cue0118848
00:13:11.234 --> 00:13:22.754
In the Egyptian culture prevailing in Canaan at that time, this means that God is the Pharaoh of the universe.

cue0118849
00:13:22.754 --> 00:13:38.354
It seems that the only viable alternative to Mesopotamian relativism was Egyptian totalitarianism.

cue0118850
00:13:38.354 --> 00:13:47.234
It must have been a very sad moment when later into the day Abraham realized the ultimate

cue0118851
00:13:47.234 --> 00:13:54.914
implication of this doctrine. A doctrine that he first visualized in his youth and

cue0118852
00:13:54.914 --> 00:14:05.034
finally understood after a long journey in his old age. When God appeared to Abraham

cue0118853
00:14:05.034 --> 00:14:26.034
At that time, that night, he began with the words, Al Tira Abram, fear not, Abram. Biblical commentators were puzzled with this assurance, as it implies that Abraham was afraid.

cue0118854
00:14:26.034 --> 00:14:33.994
What could have alarmed Abraham, wonder the commentators?

cue0118855
00:14:33.994 --> 00:14:39.494
To my mind the answer is quite obvious.

cue0118856
00:14:39.494 --> 00:14:48.714
Nothing could be more awesome and depressing than a pharaonic God ruling the universe.

cue0118857
00:14:48.714 --> 00:15:11.508
I am your shield God continues Your reward shall be very great It was a totally new perception of God I like Pharaoh the God of Abraham will be a shield to men and a source of reward and

cue0118858
00:15:11.508 --> 00:15:14.388
satisfaction.

cue0118859
00:15:14.388 --> 00:15:22.548
According to this perception, it is not sufficient to ascertain that men ought to serve God,

cue0118860
00:15:22.548 --> 00:15:28.428
But also that God wants to serve man.

cue0118861
00:15:28.428 --> 00:15:37.048
It is not surprising, therefore, to find Abraham turning to God with a question.

cue0118862
00:15:37.048 --> 00:15:44.468
God Almighty, what can you give me?

cue0118863
00:15:44.468 --> 00:15:52.008
Again, many commentators express discomfort with this question.

cue0118864
00:15:52.008 --> 00:16:02.768
In their minds, it was unacceptable that a great religious figure, let alone Abraham, would ask from God,

cue0118865
00:16:02.768 --> 00:16:09.568
what can you do for me?

cue0118866
00:16:09.568 --> 00:16:12.688
I do not agree with this view.

cue0118867
00:16:12.688 --> 00:16:19.968
The God of Abraham is not an aloof Pharaoh, high and unapproachable.

cue0118868
00:16:19.968 --> 00:16:49.948
Quite the contrary. If there is a single message that comes loud and clear throughout the scriptures is that the Almighty Lord, sole creator of the entire cosmos of galaxies and everything, the rain, in his infinite perfection, has an infinite desire to be loved and bestow his boundless goodness in his infinite perfection.

cue0118869
00:16:49.968 --> 00:17:04.908
From the pharaonic point of view, the question what can you do for me is an abomination.

cue0118870
00:17:04.908 --> 00:17:12.868
From the point of view of our Tora, it is an act of faith, an acknowledgement of God's

cue0118871
00:17:12.868 --> 00:17:16.348
infinite mercy and goodness.

cue0118872
00:17:16.348 --> 00:17:31.982
As a matter of fact is the word of God

cue0118873
00:17:54.982 --> 00:18:05.782
How dissimilar is this God from the God of Matisetic?

cue0118874
00:18:05.782 --> 00:18:13.942
Let us continue with Abraham's vision. As we know, Abraham's request from God was to

cue0118875
00:18:13.942 --> 00:18:24.962
have a child, a request that implied biological rejuvenation. It is crucial to note, therefore,

cue0118876
00:18:24.962 --> 00:18:37.962
That before granting this request, God tells Abraham, look now towards heaven and count the stars, if you be able to count them.

cue0118877
00:18:37.962 --> 00:18:43.962
And he said unto him, so shall your seed be.

cue0118878
00:18:43.962 --> 00:18:56.202
The rabbis explained that Abraham still believed in astrology, a science for which Mesopotamia

cue0118879
00:18:56.202 --> 00:19:05.602
is famous. Abraham, the rabbis continued, was convinced that the astrological configurations

cue0118880
00:19:05.602 --> 00:19:35.602
The prophets of the stars had determined that he will be childless. The rabbis ascertained that God lifted Abraham above the heavenly canopy from where he could view the entire array of the stars and told him, go away from your astrological beliefs. It is worth observing that the Hebrew

cue0118881
00:19:35.602 --> 00:19:59.976
Qote is the same term that we explain to mean go forth in freedom, be liberated. The sense of the message is quite simple. Abraham was still a prisoner of his cultural background. He needed to

cue0118882
00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:07.640
Make another step forward to finally attain freedom.

cue0118883
00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:15.720
But why was it necessary for God to lift Abraham above the celestial canopy and then challenge

cue0118884
00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:19.600
him to count the stars?

cue0118885
00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:29.800
Moreover, God's promise, so shall your seed be, seems impossible.

cue0118886
00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:40.520
The number of the stars far exceeds the population that our planet Earth can support.

cue0118887
00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:51.880
I believe that the key to the code and message of this passage lies in the word Lispor.

cue0118888
00:20:51.880 --> 00:21:04.880
This word that we translated to count is primarily an astrological term and it has the sense of configuration.

cue0118889
00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:15.720
It means to count or calibrate something within a definite frame or figure.

cue0118890
00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:30.720
American scholarship, particularly in the fields of linguistics and anthropology, have pointed out how the culture in which one lives affects the sensory perception of the individual.

cue0118891
00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:40.720
What one hears and sees is significantly determined by the culture in which one lives.

cue0118892
00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:58.720
We can easily picture Abraham ever since his youth, lifting his eyes towards heaven and actually seeing the omnius configurations of the stars announcing his impotence to the world.

cue0118893
00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:05.720
To change this perception, Abraham had to change his perspective.

cue0118894
00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:18.720
This is why God lifted Abraham above the celestial canopy in order for him to gain a total perspective of the stars.

cue0118895
00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:39.494
Can you lispor etako Chavim Can you that is to perceive the stars in their astrological configuration as the Lord The answer is obvious The patterns of perceptions imposed upon the individual by his culture become

cue0118896
00:22:39.494 --> 00:22:50.014
meaningless from the total perspective. Then and then alone Abraham completed his journey

cue0118897
00:22:50.014 --> 00:22:56.734
He was born in the city of Mesopotamia and attained genuine freedom.

cue0118898
00:22:56.734 --> 00:23:05.514
For the first time in his long life, Abraham saw the stars as they are, not according to

cue0118899
00:23:05.514 --> 00:23:12.014
some conventionalism controlling his inner perceptions.

cue0118900
00:23:12.014 --> 00:23:21.774
He was still immersed in the new view of a universe without chains when God told him,

cue0118901
00:23:21.774 --> 00:23:34.334
so shall your seed be. It was an assurance that his posterity, like him, will be able

cue0118902
00:23:34.334 --> 00:23:50.834
The children of Abraham may be enslaved and persecuted from without, but not from within.

cue0118903
00:23:50.834 --> 00:23:55.714
Our Tora make us free.

cue0118904
00:23:55.714 --> 00:24:00.354
We are almost at the end of our sermon.

cue0118905
00:24:00.354 --> 00:24:06.754
We began with Abraham's journey away from cultural bondage into spiritual freedom. We

cue0118906
00:24:06.754 --> 00:24:14.354
considered the options open to the ancient man, that of Mesopotamian relativism or Egyptian

cue0118907
00:24:14.354 --> 00:24:22.034
totalitarianism. We also touched upon Abraham's vision of religious humanism, a vision that

cue0118908
00:24:22.034 --> 00:24:27.814
was eventually fulfilled at the fault of Mount Sinai when God and the children of Israel

cue0118909
00:24:27.814 --> 00:24:34.814
People establish a Berib, covenant, determine the grounds of the relationship.

cue0118910
00:24:34.814 --> 00:24:44.934
There is a final word bearing on the character of Abraham's journey that I would like to

cue0118911
00:24:44.934 --> 00:25:10.428
share with you The parasha opens with God telling Abraham to leave his birthplace and go into the land that I will show you Elah Ares Asher Ar The Hebrew Ar has a double connotation

cue0118912
00:25:10.428 --> 00:25:16.328
It means the land which I will show you.

cue0118913
00:25:16.328 --> 00:25:21.908
But it also means the land in which I shall show you.

cue0118914
00:25:21.908 --> 00:25:27.628
In the sense of displaying Abraham to the world.

cue0118915
00:25:27.628 --> 00:25:34.628
Thus the peculiar character of Abraham's journey in our own history.

cue0118916
00:25:34.628 --> 00:25:44.628
On the one hand, God has showed us many lands throughout our long and painful history.

cue0118917
00:25:44.628 --> 00:25:51.828
We have learned much from these lands and we are grateful.

cue0118918
00:25:51.828 --> 00:26:02.228
But on the other hand, we were also displayed as something sacred and unique, a paradigm

cue0118919
00:26:02.228 --> 00:26:07.868
for mankind to emulate.

cue0118920
00:26:07.868 --> 00:26:18.668
As I look into this congregation and see Abraham's children pursuing that primeval journey, I

cue0118921
00:26:18.668 --> 00:26:22.908
I cannot help feeling overwhelmed.

cue0118922
